[
 {
  "type": "text",
  "text": "in the flow. There is also a transitional stage between laminar and turbulent flows, in which the\ndye stream will wander about and show intermittent bursts of mixing, followed by a more laminar\nbehavior.",
  "page_idx": 0,
  "bbox": [
   0.08538,
   0.07061,
   0.9144,
   0.12549
  ],
  "in_figure": false,
  "gt_note": "verif:dpbench:upstage; src=Paragraph"
 },
 {
  "type": "text",
  "text": "The Reynolds number (Re), provides a useful way of characterizing the flow. It is defined as:",
  "page_idx": 0,
  "bbox": [
   0.08783,
   0.14252,
   0.85194,
   0.16429
  ],
  "in_figure": false,
  "gt_note": "verif:dpbench:upstage; src=Paragraph"
 },
 {
  "type": "text",
  "text": "Re=\\frac{vd}{\\nu}",
  "page_idx": 0,
  "bbox": [
   0.08783,
   0.1728,
   0.17722,
   0.20024
  ],
  "in_figure": false,
  "gt_note": "verif:dpbench:upstage; src=Equation; isi berupa LaTeX"
 },
 {
  "type": "text",
  "text": "(1)",
  "page_idx": 0,
  "bbox": [
   0.21028,
   0.17848,
   0.2409,
   0.1974
  ],
  "in_figure": false,
  "gt_note": "verif:dpbench:upstage; src=Caption; caption belum ditautkan ke tabel/gambar"
 },
 {
  "type": "text",
  "text": "where (v) is the kinematic viscosity of the water (Figure 7.2), v is the mean flow velocity and d is the\ndiameter of the pipe.",
  "page_idx": 0,
  "bbox": [
   0.09028,
   0.21254,
   0.90827,
   0.25039
  ],
  "in_figure": false,
  "gt_note": "verif:dpbench:upstage; src=Paragraph"
 },
 {
  "type": "text",
  "text": "The Reynolds number is a dimensionless parameter thatis the ratio of the inertial (destabilizing) force\nto the viscosity (stabilizing) force. As Re increases, the inertial force becomes relatively larger, and the\nflow destabilizes and becomes fully turbulent.",
  "page_idx": 0,
  "bbox": [
   0.08293,
   0.26364,
   0.91562,
   0.32136
  ],
  "in_figure": false,
  "gt_note": "verif:dpbench:upstage; src=Paragraph"
 },
 {
  "type": "text",
  "text": "The Reynolds experiment determines the critical Reynolds number for pipe flow at which laminar\nflow (Re<2000) becomes transitional (2000<Re<4000) and the transitional flow becomes turbulent\n(Re>4000). The advantage of using a critical Reynolds number, instead of critical velocity, is that the\nresults of the experiments are applicable to all Newtonian fluid flows in pipes with a circular cross-\nsection.",
  "page_idx": 0,
  "bbox": [
   0.0866,
   0.33366,
   0.91072,
   0.42923
  ],
  "in_figure": false,
  "gt_note": "verif:dpbench:upstage; src=Paragraph"
 },
